    "assumptions": "A is a complex abelian sixfold, M is a degree-12 CM field acting with H1(A,Q) rank one over M, and T=Hg(A) is its connected Hodge torus. Over a characteristic-zero splitting field F, the twelve embedding lines have weights +w_i,-w_i for i=1,...,6. The map Q^6 to X*(T) tensor Q taking e_i to w_i has rank 5 and kernel Q*r, with every r_i in {+1,-1}. The two sign-selected embedding supports S and conjugate(S) are fibers of restriction of embeddings M to an imaginary quadratic K. These assumptions are verified at abstract type level by the inspected census, not a curve-realization claim.",
    "proof_steps": [
      "Hg(A^n) acts diagonally as Hg(A), because the direct-sum weight-one Hodge structure has the same defining Hodge circle and its smallest Q-algebraic group. After extending scalars to F, H1(A^n,F) has n copies of each embedding eigenline. Its exterior monomials form a weight basis. A monomial is invariant exactly when its total T-character vanishes.",
      "For an invariant monomial let a_i and b_i be the numbers of chosen copies of weights w_i and -w_i. Cancel min(a_i,b_i) opposite-weight pairs separately for each i. Their degree-two wedges are invariant. For the residual monomial d_i=a_i-b_i satisfies sum_i d_i w_i=0, so d=q*r for rational q. Since d is integral and r_1 is a unit integer, q is an integer. The character lattice of a torus has no torsion, so no additional torsion-weight condition appears.",
      "If q=0 the monomial was entirely a product of quadratic invariants. If q>0 the residual monomial has q chosen copies of weight r_i*w_i for each i and can be partitioned into q disjoint six-wedges, each taking one copy of all six selected embedding lines. For q<0 use the conjugate support. The actual indices of the selected copies can be partitioned arbitrarily; exterior reordering contributes only an irrelevant sign. This proves generation over F by invariant quadratics and the 2*n^6 independent six-wedges.",
      "W_K(A) is a rational subspace: over F it is the sum of the top exterior lines of the two K-eigenspaces in H1(A,F), and Gal(F/Q) preserves this pair. Equivalently it is the usual rational Weil subspace obtained from the K-linear top exterior power, with the embedding into Q-exterior cohomology interpreted through this split description. The balanced signatures make these lines Hodge classes. No individual split eigenvector is claimed rational.",
      "Fix a rational order O contained in End(A) intersect M. Such an order exists for every rational M-action, by clearing denominators in a Z-basis of M. For a=(a_1,...,a_n) in O^n, the homomorphism f_a:A^n to A sends (x_j) to sum_j a_j x_j. If e_sigma is an embedding eigenvector, f_a^*e_sigma=sum_j sigma(a_j)e_sigma,j. Hence the pullback of the S-Weil line is wedge_{sigma in S}(sum_j sigma(a_j)e_sigma,j). Its polynomial coefficient for product_{sigma in S} z_sigma,j_sigma is exactly the six-wedge with copy assignment sigma to j_sigma. The same holds for conjugate(S).",
      "O^n is Zariski dense in the Q-vector space M^n even after extending to F: choose a Z-basis of O, then a polynomial over F vanishing on the lattice Z^(12n) is zero by induction on the number of variables, since a nonzero one-variable polynomial cannot have infinitely many integer roots. The embedding-coordinate map M tensor F to F^12 is an invertible F-linear map. Consequently the displayed coefficient polynomials have no relations caused by restriction to O^n.",
